SPORT_EXPLAINERS = [

("the-offside-rule-explained", "The offside rule, explained like a friend would",
"Position, timing and involvement: the three ideas that unlock football's most-argued law — and why assistants now let play breathe before flagging.",
"""<p>Offside confuses people for one reason: it is three rules wearing one shirt. Learn the three ideas and the arguments mostly evaporate.</p>
<h2>Idea one: position</h2>
<p>You are in an offside <em>position</em> if, at the moment a teammate plays the ball to you, any part of you that can legally score is closer to the opponent's goal line than both the ball and the second-last defender. Read that again slowly: the goalkeeper usually counts as one of the two defenders, so the working version is &ldquo;level with the last defender or the ball is safe.&rdquo; Level is onside &mdash; the benefit of the doubt is written into the law itself. Hands and arms do not count for either side; you cannot be offside on your arm and you cannot play a defender onside with theirs.</p>
<h2>Idea two: timing</h2>
<p>The judgement moment is when the pass is <em>played</em>, not when it arrives. A striker drifting beyond the defence is perfectly legal if they were behind the line when their teammate struck the ball. This is why offside arguments are really arguments about freeze-frames, and why television draws lines for a living.</p>
<h2>Idea three: involvement</h2>
<p>Being in an offside position is not an offence. It becomes one only when you get involved: interfering with play, interfering with an opponent (a defender's ability to play the ball &mdash; including being in their sightline), or gaining an advantage from that position, such as playing a rebound off the post. A player standing offside and silent while the ball goes elsewhere has done nothing wrong. This is the part casual watchers miss, and it is why assistant referees hold their flags: if the offside player never touches the ball, the flag should stay down and play should breathe.</p>
<h2>The modern wrinkles, honestly dated</h2>
<p>Two things make offside feel different from a decade ago. Semi-automated offside technology at the biggest competitions tracks the freeze-frame with sensors and draws the line for the officials &mdash; faster, and arguable in a new way (a toe's width, measured precisely). And interpretations get clarified over time: in 2023 top leagues agreed to flag only once a player actually touches the ball, formalising the &ldquo;wait and see&rdquo; practice. The core law is stable; the margins are engineered. When a decision confuses you, ask which of the three ideas is being argued &mdash; position, timing or involvement &mdash; and the argument usually names itself.</p>""",
[("IFAB — Laws of the Game (Law 11, Offside)", "https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/offside/")],
[("how-var-works", "How VAR actually works"),
 ("promotion-and-relegation-explained", "Promotion and relegation, explained")]),

("promotion-and-relegation-explained", "Promotion and relegation: the idea that makes football's pyramid breathe",
"Why clubs can rise and fall between leagues, why relegation hurts more than losing, and why the big American leagues do things differently.",
"""<p>Promotion and relegation is football's open-door policy: every season, the worst teams in a league drop to the division below, and the best teams below rise. No vote, no invitation, no franchise protection. It is the reason a bottom-of-the-table match in April can matter more than a title race.</p>
<h2>The pyramid in one picture</h2>
<p>English football is the clearest example of a pyramid: the Premier League at the top, the Championship below it, then League One and League Two, then the National League, and downwards through hundreds of interconnected tiers &mdash; all the way to village football, where the same rules of ascent technically apply. Each summer the pieces shift: three clubs go up from the Championship to the Premier League, three come down; the same exchange happens at every rung (with playoffs deciding some promotion places, because a knockout fight for the last seat is worth more to everyone than a table that has stopped moving).</p>
<h2>Why relegation is not just &ldquo;losing&rdquo;</h2>
<p>Relegation is financial as much as sporting. Top-flight clubs share broadcasting money that dwarfs the divisions below; dropping out shrinks income overnight while contracts signed at top-flight rates keep running. That is why parachute payments exist &mdash; a cushion for relegated clubs, designed to keep them from collapse and debated endlessly for distorting the division they land in. On the pitch, relegation often means selling best players to balance books, which is why promoted clubs spend heavily immediately and why the January transfer window has a relegation six-pointer economy of its own.</p>
<h2>The counter-model, fairly stated</h2>
<p>The big American leagues (NFL, NBA, MLB) run closed franchises: the same teams every year, no relegation, the league as a business partnership of fixed members. Bad seasons cost draft position, not membership. The honest comparison: closed leagues trade sporting jeopardy for financial stability; the pyramid trades stability for the possibility that a hundred-year-old club from a small town can, in principle, climb to the top. Both systems produce drama &mdash; only one of them can produce Leicester City winning the Premier League a year after nearly going down, or Wigan Athletic reaching the Premier League from the fourth tier within a decade. That possibility is the product.</p>""",
[],
[("the-offside-rule-explained", "The offside rule, explained"),
 ("why-football-transfers-collapse", "Why transfers collapse")]),

("how-var-works", "How VAR actually works &mdash; and why it still annoys everyone",
"What the video officials check, what they may not touch, why decisions take so long, and the honest trade-off the whole thing bought.",
"""<p>Video Assistant Referee is a support system for the on-field referee, not a parallel authority. That single sentence explains most of what people find baffling about it &mdash; the delays, the permissions, and the decisions that end with a shrug toward the pitch.</p>
<h2>What VAR is allowed to check</h2>
<p>Four categories, and only four: goals (and anything in the build-up that would cancel one), penalty decisions, direct red cards, and mistaken identity (the wrong player booked or sent off). Everything else &mdash; yellow cards, fouls outside those moments, the throw-in that went the wrong way &mdash; is invisible to VAR by design. The system was built to correct obvious errors in the moments that decide matches, not to re-referee ninety minutes.</p>
<h2>The process, step by step</h2>
<p>Every checkable incident gets checked silently: the VAR team reviews footage while play continues. Three outcomes. <b>Check complete</b> &mdash; nothing to see, play moves on, and you often never learn a check happened. <b>Recommendation to review</b> &mdash; the VAR flags a possible clear error; the referee can act on the tip or look themselves at a pitchside screen. <b>On-field review</b> &mdash; the referee walks to the monitor, watches the replays, and makes the final call personally. That last point is the architecture: the video officials recommend, the referee decides. When a goal is ruled out after a review and fans say &ldquo;the computer did that,&rdquo; it did not &mdash; a human on the pitch did, with better views than they had.</p>
<h2>Why it takes so long &mdash; and the offside exception</h2>
<p>The threshold for intervention is &ldquo;clear and obvious error&rdquo;, and judgement calls (was that enough contact for a penalty?) are deliberately hard to overturn, which produces long pauses that end in the original decision. Offside is different: it is factual, and at the biggest competitions semi-automated technology generates the lines from tracking data, which is why offside reviews can be quicker than judgement reviews &mdash; and why a goal can be cancelled by a toe. For the law those lines are measuring, see <a href="/the-offside-rule-explained/">the offside rule explained</a>.</p>
<h2>The honest trade-off</h2>
<p>VAR traded flow for accuracy, and everyone agrees both halves of that sentence. More decisions are correct; every decision now arrives with a pause and a debate about the process. The leagues keep tuning the experience &mdash; faster checks, more transparency in stadiums &mdash; but the core bargain stands: the moments that decide matches now get a second look, and the second look is here to stay.</p>""",
[("IFAB — Laws of the Game (Video match officials)", "https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/law-5-the-referee/")],
[("the-offside-rule-explained", "The offside rule, explained"),
 ("promotion-and-relegation-explained", "Promotion and relegation, explained")]),

("why-football-transfers-collapse", "Why football transfers collapse (it is rarely the fee)",
"Agreement, terms, medical, paperwork: four doors between a done deal and a done deal — and the transfer window's favourite tragedies live in the gaps.",
"""<p>The most reliable rule of the transfer window: a &ldquo;done deal&rdquo; is not done until the player is holding the shirt. Clubs can agree a fee and still watch the move die for reasons that had nothing to do with money. Understanding the pipeline explains the window's strangest weekends.</p>
<h2>The four doors</h2>
<p><b>1. The fee.</b> Buying and selling clubs agree a price &mdash; sometimes add-ons, sell-on clauses, loan-backs. This is the part that fills the news, and it is the most likely stage to succeed. <b>2. Personal terms.</b> The player's side negotiates wages, length, bonuses, and &mdash; the quiet one &mdash; the project: playing time, role, city, manager. Plenty of transfers die here with the fee already settled, because the selling club's agreement is not the player's promise. <b>3. The medical.</b> The club's doctors examine the player; a flag on an old injury, a recurring muscle issue, or a cardiac finding can shrink the deal, convert it to a loan with obligations, or end it in an afternoon. Medicals are private, which is why collapses that cite them feel mysterious from outside. <b>4. The paperwork.</b> International clearance, contracts lodged with the league and the federation before the deadline, work permits where immigration rules apply. Deadline day exists as theatre precisely because this stage has a hard stop: a deal that misses the paperwork window does not go through &ldquo;a bit late&rdquo;. It dies.</p>
<h2>The pressures that bend all four</h2>
<p>Agents move between multiple suitors, and a player &ldquo;in talks&rdquo; with three clubs is sometimes choosing, sometimes leveraging. Selling clubs time announcements to secure replacements first, which is why agreed deals sit unsigned for days. And everywhere, the wage structure: clubs will break their budget for a fee far more readily than their dressing room's pay scale, because the fee is paid once and the wages argue with every teammate for four years.</p>
<h2>Why the collapsed ones stay famous</h2>
<p>A failed megadeal has everything a football story needs &mdash; a medical that went wrong in a private room, a flight that turned around, a photo taken an hour too soon. Our <a href="/premier-league-transfer-tracker-august-2026/">August 2026 transfer tracker</a> and <a href="/deadline-day-dont-try-to-make-sense-of-it/">deadline-day field guide</a> are archive editions of exactly this theatre. And the modern version of the pipeline has a new actor at the top of it: <a href="/what-does-a-sporting-director-do/">the sporting director</a>, whose job is making doors two through four boring.</p>""",
[],
[("what-does-a-sporting-director-do", "What a sporting director does"),
 ("premier-league-transfer-tracker-august-2026", "August 2026 transfer tracker (archive)"),
 ("deadline-day-dont-try-to-make-sense-of-it", "Deadline day: don't try to make sense of it (archive)")]),

("what-does-a-sporting-director-do", "What does a sporting director actually do?",
"Modern clubs increasingly answer to two voices: the manager picks the team, and someone above him picks the machine that picks the players.",
"""<p>Twenty years ago, most football clubs had one football authority: the manager, who signed players, coached them and answered for results. The modern structure is different, and the job title at its centre &mdash; sporting director, technical director, director of football, depending on the club's mood &mdash; is the most misunderstood role in the game.</p>
<h2>The job in one sentence</h2>
<p>The sporting director owns the football project's coherence; the manager owns the team. In practice that means the director builds the recruitment machine &mdash; scouting networks, data models, target lists by position and age profile &mdash; negotiates the club's approach to contracts and sales, manages the academy pathway to the first team, and hires (and sometimes fires) managers to fit a style of play the club has decided to pursue, rather than rebuilding the squad every time the dugout changes.</p>
<h2>Why clubs created the role</h2>
<p>Because the old model had a failure mode: a manager leaves, and the next one wants different players. Three managers and three philosophies later, the club owns six strikers who fit nobody's system, on contracts nobody wants. A sporting director is the continuity argument &mdash; the person whose plan outlives the man in the dugout. It also splits a job that quietly became two: coaching a squad daily and building a squad across years are different full-time skills, and the best managers are rarely the best recruiters.</p>
<h2>The friction, honestly stated</h2>
<p>Where the manager stops and the director starts is the argument. A manager told &ldquo;these are your three striker options&rdquo; may want the fourth; a director protecting a wage structure will veto him, and the press conference will be asked about it by Sunday. Some pairings thrive on the division of labour; others read like a divorce reported through transfer gossip. The role works when the club's owners define the boundary clearly and back one of the two voices when they disagree &mdash; and fails, publicly, when they do not.</p>
<h2>Why it matters to fans</h2>
<p>When your club signs a 22-year-old from a league nobody scouted, that is the director's machine working; when it panic-buys a 32-year-old on deadline day, that is the machine failing or being ignored. The pipeline that produced it, and the doors a transfer must survive, are in <a href="/why-football-transfers-collapse/">why transfers collapse</a>.</p>""",
[],
[("why-football-transfers-collapse", "Why transfers collapse"),
 ("the-offside-rule-explained", "The offside rule, explained"),
 ("promotion-and-relegation-explained", "Promotion and relegation, explained")]),

("why-does-afcon-move-around", "Why does AFCON move around the calendar?",
"The Africa Cup of Nations bounces between January and summer &mdash; climate, calendars and television all pull at it. Here is the honest story.",
"""<p>No major tournament changes dates like the Africa Cup of Nations. It has been played in January, February, and mid-year; it has overlapped with European seasons and sat inside summer breaks; and every move sets off the same debate about clubs losing players mid-season. The bouncing is not chaos &mdash; it is a real negotiation between forces that genuinely conflict.</p>
<h2>The forces pulling the dates</h2>
<p><b>Climate.</b> Some host countries are brutally hot in June and July; a summer edition there means midday kick-offs in dangerous heat, so a cooler-month tournament is better for players and crowds. <b>The European calendar.</b> Most African internationals play for European clubs, and a mid-season AFCON removes them for three-to-four weeks plus a preparation camp &mdash; which is why European clubs have lobbied for a summer slot for decades, and why January editions feel like an unpaid tax on African squads. <b>Broadcast value.</b> A tournament against no major competition earns more than one buried beside a European championship or a World Cup summer. And <b>politics and practicality</b>: hosting rights have changed hands for readiness reasons, which pushes dates with them.</p>
<h2>Where it has landed lately</h2>
<p>The pattern of recent years: tournaments scheduled for the northern-hemisphere winter when the host's climate demands it &mdash; Cameroon's edition was played in January&ndash;February 2022, Ivory Coast's in January&ndash;February 2024 &mdash; and the Morocco edition shifted further into December 2025 to January 2026, easing the club-release clash by riding the European winter break. The 2027 edition is set for East Africa, hosted by Kenya, Tanzania and Uganda. Every choice solved one problem and sharpened another; that is the honest summary of AFCON scheduling.</p>
<h2>The part worth defending</h2>
<p>The January tournament has one underrated virtue: it occupies the African football stage alone. No World Cup to share a summer with, no European championship on the other channel &mdash; for a month, the best players on the continent are the main event globally, not a side story. Whether the calendar keeps landing there depends on which force wins the next round: the clubs, the broadcasters, or the climate. If you are new to the tournament, that is also the practical tip: check the dates edition by edition, because unlike almost every other major tournament, AFCON's place in the calendar is a decision, not a tradition.</p>""",
[("CAF — TotalEnergies Africa Cup of Nations", "https://www.cafonline.com/")],
[("promotion-and-relegation-explained", "Promotion and relegation, explained"),
 ("how-var-works", "How VAR actually works"),
 ("the-offside-rule-explained", "The offside rule, explained")]),

("how-the-champions-league-works", "How the Champions League works, from entry to the final",
"The whole journey in one piece: who gets in, the eight-game league phase, the playoffs, and why the final is one match on one night.",
"""<p>The UEFA Champions League is European club football's championship, and since 2024-25 it runs on a format that even regular watchers are still learning. Here is the whole journey, stage by stage.</p>
<h2>Who gets in</h2>
<p>Thirty-six clubs qualify each season. Most places are earned through league positions in the previous season's domestic campaigns &mdash; the strongest countries send several clubs, smaller leagues send their champions through qualifying rounds that start in high summer. The title holders qualify automatically, and UEFA publishes the exact access list every season, because slot allocation shifts with coefficient rankings and rule changes. That is why qualification night matters across the whole continent: a league position in May can be worth a Champions League summer.</p>
<h2>The league phase: eight games, one table</h2>
<p>Instead of the old eight groups of four, all 36 clubs now sit in one league table. Each club plays eight matches &mdash; eight different opponents, two drawn from each of four seeding pots, four home games and four away. It runs from September to late January, and every result moves you up or down one shared table, which is why a goal in the 94th minute in January can swing four clubs at once.</p>
<h2>What the table buys</h2>
<p>Finish in the top eight and you go straight to the round of 16. Finish 9th to 24th and you earn a two-legged knockout playoff in February against another side from that band &mdash; win it and you join the top eight in the round of 16. Finish 25th or lower and you are out of Europe entirely: under the new format there is no safety net dropping you into the Europa League, which the old third-place group stage offered. From the round of 16 on, it is the classic two-legged ties, seeded so that higher league-phase finishers avoid each other until later rounds, ending in a one-off final at a venue chosen years in advance &mdash; late May or early June, one night, no second chance.</p>
<h2>Why this format exists</h2>
<p>UEFA's stated aims were more guaranteed big-club meetings and a fairer route past the group stage; the trade, contested loudly, is four more matches on player calendars and a longer elite season. Our <a href="/sports/champions-league-new-format-explained/">old-versus-new comparison</a> walks through exactly what changed and why people argue about it. For the law of the game itself rather than the competition, see <a href="/sports/the-offside-rule-explained/">the offside rule explained</a>.</p>""",
[("UEFA — UEFA Champions League", "https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/")],
[("champions-league-new-format-explained", "The new format, old vs new"),
 ("the-offside-rule-explained", "The offside rule, explained")]),

("champions-league-new-format-explained", "The Champions League's new format: what actually changed",
"36 teams, one table, eight games each. Here is the old format, the new one, and the honest case each side makes.",
"""<p>In 2024-25 the Champions League replaced the format it had used for over two decades. If the new one confuses you, the fastest way in is to see what was thrown away.</p>
<h2>The old world: eight groups of four</h2>
<p>Thirty-two teams, eight groups, home and away against three opponents each, six matches total. Top two advanced to the round of 16; the third place dropped into the Europa League knockout rounds; the bottom two went home. The known quirks: the same six opponents every autumn, and the strange spectacle of a giant finishing third and continuing its European season in a lesser competition.</p>
<h2>The new world: one league of 36</h2>
<p>All 36 clubs in a single table. Eight matches, eight different opponents, two from each of four pots, four home and four away, September to January. The rewards are tiered: top eight straight to the round of 16; ninth through 24th into February playoffs for the remaining eight places; 25th onward are eliminated outright &mdash; no Europa League parachute anymore. The knockout rounds are bracketed by league position, so winning the league phase genuinely matters: it buys an easier road right up to the semi-finals.</p>
<h2>The case for, and against &mdash; honestly</h2>
<p>For: guaranteed meetings between the biggest clubs in the league phase (under group formats, giants could dodge each other until spring), more matches for broadcasters and fans, and a table that keeps every matchday live &mdash; no dead rubbers by week five. Against: four extra matches on calendars that players' unions already call overloaded, a format whose arithmetic (pots, brackets, seeding) is harder for a casual fan to hold, and the loss of the group stage's simple tradition: six games, three opponents, top two through. Both cases are sincere; the format is young enough that its folklore &mdash; the dramatic final-matchday table reshuffles &mdash; is still being written.</p>
<h2>The one thing that did not change</h2>
<p>The final: one match, one night, a venue booked years ahead. Everything before it got more complicated; the last game stayed simple. For the whole journey from qualification to that night, see <a href="/sports/how-the-champions-league-works/">how the Champions League works</a> &mdash; and for how the calendar collision works when Africa's championship meets Europe's, our <a href="/sports/why-does-afcon-move-around/">AFCON calendar explainer</a> covers the other side of that tug-of-war.</p>""",
[("UEFA — UEFA Champions League", "https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/")],
[("how-the-champions-league-works", "How the Champions League works"),
 ("why-does-afcon-move-around", "Why AFCON moves around")]),
]

